"93_apppixels.mst — AppSpec A3: a pane's pixels, portably.

 `docs/appspec.md`. A pane is the one node whose contents no diff can carry:
 its props say where it is and how big, and everything INSIDE it is pixels the
 tool computes. So the tool needs a way to write them, and that way has to be
 the same sentence on both platforms or the portability claim stops at the
 first tool that draws — which is every interesting one.

 THE SHAPE IS WINARMVM'S, because a shape already in use by a working tool
 beats a nicer one invented here. Its sprite editor says:

     base := WinPixels planeFor: hwnd width: gw height: gh.
     stride := WinPixels strideFor: hwnd.
     WinPixels at: base x: px y: py stride: stride put:
         (WinPixels bgraR: r g: g b: b).
     WinRender present: hwnd.

 so `AppPixels` and `AppRender` answer exactly those, and
 `world/90_appspec_compat.mst` aliases the Win names onto them. A tool written
 against either name draws on both platforms.

 WHAT IS PORTABLE AND WHAT IS NOT. The PLANE is portable: a ByteArray of
 32-bit pixels the tool pokes, retained between paints so a partial repaint is
 sound rather than merely fast. PRESENTING it is not, so a realizer hands this
 registry a block that knows how to get bytes onto its own screen — a base64
 blast through the canvas host verb here, a D3D11 present there. Nothing in
 this file mentions either.

 BGRA, NOT RGBA, and it is worth being explicit because the name of the
 selector is the only warning: `bgraR:g:b:` packs a word in the order Windows'
 own plane wants. The Mac's presenter unpacks it back to RGBA on its way out,
 which costs one shuffle per pixel in the blast and keeps every TOOL's
 arithmetic identical across the family. The alternative — two spellings of
 `bgraR:g:b:` — would put a per-platform branch inside every tool that draws,
 which is exactly what this framework exists to remove."

Object subclass: AppSurface [
    | handle width height plane presenter |

    AppSurface class >> handle: h <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: hh <Integer>
                        present: aBlock <Object> ^ <AppSurface> [
        ^self new setHandle: h width: w height: hh present: aBlock
    ]

    setHandle: h <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: hh <Integer>
    present: aBlock <Object> ^ <AppSurface> [
        handle := h.
        presenter := aBlock.
        ^self resizeTo: w height: hh
    ]

    "THE PLANE IS RETAINED between paints — that is the fact that makes a
     damage rect sound. Reallocated only when the size actually changes,
     because a fresh buffer would silently discard everything outside the
     damaged region and turn a partial repaint into a mostly-blank one."
    resizeTo: w <Integer> height: hh <Integer> ^ <AppSurface> [
        (plane isNil or: [ width ~= w or: [ height ~= hh ] ]) ifTrue: [
            width := w max: 1.
            height := hh max: 1.
            plane := ByteArray new: width * height * 4 ].
        ^self
    ]

    handle ^ <Integer> [ ^handle ]
    width ^ <Integer> [ ^width ]
    height ^ <Integer> [ ^height ]
    plane ^ <ByteArray> [ ^plane ]
    present [ presenter isNil ifFalse: [ presenter value: self ]. ^self ]
]

"The handle -> surface map. A realizer fills it in as it creates panes; a tool
 reaches it only through AppPixels/AppRender, and by handle, so nothing above
 this line has to know which realizer made the pane."
Object subclass: AppSurfaces [
    <classVars: Surfaces NextHandle>

    AppSurfaces class >> all ^ <Dictionary> [
        Surfaces isNil ifTrue: [ Surfaces := Dictionary new ].
        ^Surfaces
    ]

    AppSurfaces class >> nextHandle ^ <Integer> [
        NextHandle isNil ifTrue: [ NextHandle := 0 ].
        NextHandle := NextHandle + 1.
        ^NextHandle
    ]

    AppSurfaces class >> register: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> present: aBlock <Object> ^ <Integer> [
        | handle |
        handle := self nextHandle.
        self all at: handle put:
            (AppSurface handle: handle width: w height: h present: aBlock).
        ^handle
    ]

    AppSurfaces class >> at: handle <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        ^self all at: handle ifAbsent: [ nil ]
    ]

    AppSurfaces class >> remove: handle <Integer> [
        self all removeKey: handle ifAbsent: [ nil ].
        ^self
    ]

    AppSurfaces class >> reset [ Surfaces := nil. NextHandle := nil. ^self ]
]

"The tool-facing pixel verbs. Class-side and handle-keyed, exactly as
 WINARMVM's are, so a tool's drawing loop is the same text on both platforms."
Object subclass: AppPixels [

    "Answer the plane to poke, sized to what the caller expects. A pane whose
     node changed size gets a new buffer here rather than at creation, which
     is how a tool that recomputes its own geometry stays correct."
    AppPixels class >> planeFor: handle <Integer> width: w <Integer> height: h <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        | s |
        s := AppSurfaces at: handle.
        s isNil ifTrue: [ ^nil ].
        s resizeTo: w height: h.
        ^s plane
    ]

    AppPixels class >> strideFor: handle <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | s |
        s := AppSurfaces at: handle.
        s isNil ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^s width * 4
    ]

    "A pixel as one 32-bit word, B G R A in ascending byte order — Windows'
     own plane order, kept so a tool's constants never need translating."
    AppPixels class >> bgraR: r <Integer> g: g <Integer> b: b <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        ^(b bitAnd: 255)
            + ((g bitAnd: 255) bitShift: 8)
            + ((r bitAnd: 255) bitShift: 16)
            + (255 bitShift: 24)
    ]

    "Write one word. Out-of-bounds is IGNORED rather than raised: a paint
     block runs inside an event, and a tool that miscomputes one edge pixel
     should draw the other 65,535 rather than take the window down."
    AppPixels class >> at: plane <Object> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                       stride: stride <Integer> put: word <Integer> [
        | i |
        plane isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (x < 0 or: [ y < 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        i := (y * stride) + (x * 4).
        (i < 0 or: [ i + 4 > plane size ]) ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        plane at: i + 1 put: (word bitAnd: 255).
        plane at: i + 2 put: ((word bitShift: -8) bitAnd: 255).
        plane at: i + 3 put: ((word bitShift: -16) bitAnd: 255).
        plane at: i + 4 put: ((word bitShift: -24) bitAnd: 255).
        ^self
    ]

    "Read one back — for tests, which is the only honest way to assert that a
     paint block wrote what it meant to without looking at a screenshot."
    AppPixels class >> at: plane <Object> x: x <Integer> y: y <Integer>
                       stride: stride <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | i |
        plane isNil ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        i := (y * stride) + (x * 4).
        (i < 0 or: [ i + 4 > plane size ]) ifTrue: [ ^0 ].
        ^(plane at: i + 1)
            + ((plane at: i + 2) bitShift: 8)
            + ((plane at: i + 3) bitShift: 16)
            + ((plane at: i + 4) bitShift: 24)
    ]
]

"Getting a plane onto the screen. One verb that matters, and the realizer
 supplied the block that performs it."
Object subclass: AppRender [

    AppRender class >> present: handle <Integer> [
        | s |
        s := AppSurfaces at: handle.
        s isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        s present.
        ^self
    ]

    "A pane's size, for a tool that wants to fit its drawing to it."
    AppRender class >> widthFor: handle <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | s |
        s := AppSurfaces at: handle.
        ^s isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ s width ]
    ]

    AppRender class >> heightFor: handle <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | s |
        s := AppSurfaces at: handle.
        ^s isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ s height ]
    ]
]
